00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:05.059 align:start position:0%
on May 19th 2015 CD Projekt RED released  what many considered to be one of the  best role-playing games of all time The  Witcher 3 Wild Hunt 5 years later as we  usher in a new console generation let's  explore what made this game a remarkable  achievement and a powerhouse in the  video game industry  [Music]  now before we look at the game itself  it's important to set the stage the Xbox  one and ps4 had been on the market for  roughly a year and a half and while  there were some great games for our  shiny new consoles nothing felt truly  next-gen part of this was due to the  fact that most games were cross-gen  meaning they launched on ps3 and xbox  360 as well as ps4 and Xbox one even the  best games of 2014 like Dragon Age  Inquisition and titanfall were designed  with the old tech in mind meanwhile the  games made specifically for Xbox one and  ps4  felt more like ambitious tech demos than  full fledged games or they were just  really buggy cross-gen games are pretty  common at the start of a new generation  but there was a dreaded new trend that  was starting to become commonplace in  Triple A games microtransactions  microtransactions were nothing new when  it came to mobile games but they were  rapidly popping up in triple-a  experiences Microsoft even made it  crystal clear that every one of its  first party games for the foreseeable  future would have in-game purchases  fortunately if dears tactics is any  indication they may have eased off that  policy now you're probably wondering why  I'm talking about microtransactions in a  video about The Witcher 3 but bear with  me while this was all happening a modest  studio in Poland was hard at work on the  final installment of their mature  fantasy series unlike many of the games  released in 2013 and 2014 The Witcher 3  Wild Hunt was built from the ground up  to fully utilize the new consoles CD  Projekt RED wanted to create a world as  detailed as it was big with a lengthy  branching narrative that would keep  players invested for dozens if not  hundreds of hours if that wasn't enough  they made it abundantly clear that there  would be no microtransactions and all  small DLC packs would be entirely free I  really think the word DLC it's evaluated  there are some new C's which are like  good old expansions and then I'll  probably prefer to call them expansion  yeah if it's a DLC if it's  hairstyle weapon armoire horse armor  maybe and this should be the city for  free me although not every Triple A game  at the time had microtransactions it was  refreshing to see a developer like CD  Projekt RED takes such a firm stance  against in-game purchases however none  of that would matter if The Witcher 3  Wild Hunt didn't meet fan expectations  [Music]  on May 19 2015 after a couple delays The  Witcher 3 launched on PlayStation 4 Xbox  one and PC 2 overwhelmingly positive  reviews GameSpot's Kevin Bay Nord  awarded it a 10 saying excellence of  bounds at every turn in this open-world  role-playing game excellent exploration  excellent creature design excellent  combat mechanics and excellent player  progression  despite the Witcher threes unprecedented  scale there was still so much care put  into the finer details from the side  quest to the optional monster hunts  everything felt weighty and worthwhile  thanks to the jaw-dropping presentation  and sharp writing rarely if ever did  Strang off the beaten path feel like a  waste of time there is always something  new to learn and explore what still  impresses me to this day is how complex  and well-thought-out the narrative is  there weren't just good and bad  decisions with obvious consequences  instead CD Projekt RED went a subtler  route seemingly small decisions could  have big repercussions both good and bad  no matter what you did you'd still  second-guess yourself the glue that kept  everything together though were the  game's vivid and fantastic characters  even beyond the core cast there were so  many excellent side characters like the  bloody baron Johnny Lambert Keira Metz  and so on  financially The Witcher 3 was also a  huge success selling over 6 million  copies in the first six weeks and over  20 million as of 2019 the success didn't  go unnoticed either many developers have  taken inspiration from The Witcher 3  most notably Ubisoft Assassin's Creed  series Assassin's Creed origins marked a  fresh start for the series and with that  we saw many nods to CD Projekt Reds  open-world RPG such as the quest  progression and loot system the only  thing missing was a branching narrative  which appeared in the next  Assassin's Creed Odyssey the success of  The Witcher 3 also had massive  repercussions for CD Projekt RED the  team greatly expanded both in head count  and office space opening a new studio in  Poland a few years later GOG CD Projekt  Reds PC games storefront took a  respectable market share from Steam  putting their DRM policy front and  center and of course I think it's safe  to say that the witcher 3 success has  influenced cyberpunk 2077 s development  in some way or another it's impossible  to say exactly what cyberpunk 2077 would  look like had the witcher 3 flopped but  i'd imagine the final product would be  very different  and of course i'd be willing to bet that  if the witcher 3 wasn't as successful as  it was the netflix witcher show probably  wouldn't exist while I do have some  qualms with the show there's no denying  the impact it had never in my wildest  dreams that I think my parents would be  chatting about Geralt of Rivia and  Cyrilla Fiona Ellen Rhiannon around the  dinner table but here we are and I  couldn't be happier about it as always  thank you so much for watching and let  us know what your favorite moments are  in The Witcher 3 down below we'll see  you next time  [Applause]  you
 

